EL is an inexpressive DL only allowing cojunctions n and ∃ as constructors.

Syntax of EL :
The main blocks of EL are Concepts and Roles
Concepts are unary predicates and roles are binary predicates. Expresses relationship between objects
Here we ignore constants, only use of variables

We consider infinite disjoint sets; 
Nc  for concept names such as female
Nr  for role names such as haschild
Formally they are unbounded and infinite

Concepts are always represented as capital letters (A,B,C) where roles are in simple letters (r,s,t)

EL⊥ concepts are defined by (A € Nc (concept name), r € Nr(role name)
C :: = A | T | ⊥ | C ∏ C | ∃ r.C
C: class of all concepts concepts names, sometimes are called complex concepts to differentiate from concept names
Bars (|) separate different possibility
A: concept name
T: tautology (always true)
⊥ : contradiction
C: is a general concept, the two C can be different

There can exist the class of all concept names, tautologies and contradictions are concepts. 




There can be the conjunction of two concept names C and there can be r as a role name and ∃ r.C is a concept
- Female is a concept
- T is a concept
- ∃ hasChild.Female (hasChild is a role name, Female is a concept, so ∃ hasChild.Female is a concert)
- Female ∏ ∃ hasChild.T it is a concept. Refers to the class that have a female class
- ∃ hasChild.Female ∏ ∃ hasChild.Male (Male is a concept, that total is a concept). Class of people that have one female and one male
- ∃ hasChild.(Female ∏ ∃ hasChild.Male) is a concept . People that have a child that is a female that have a male child. Have a daughter and that daughter have a son
- ∃hasChid.⊥ is a concept (it actually is a contraction) people that have a child that cannot exists

