Let C and D be EL concepts
C is subsumed by D if for all intepretations I C is subsumed by D meaning C is a sub class of DWe will denote this as C ⊑ D
Concept trees : 
 
This is sort of a canonical model

Subsumption through homomorphism : 
A Homomorphism between two concept trees T1 T2 is a function h mapping each node from T1 to T2
h(root(T1)=root(T2))
For every n € nodes(T1), label(n) ⊆ label(h(n))
If m is an r-successor of n, then h(m) is an r-successor of h(n)

Every element belongs to both trees
If the node is A then it needs to be mapped to at least A
If A was an r successor of B then it should be same for h(A) as an r successor of h(B)
Remember! C ⊑ D does not mean D ⊑ C

C is subsumed by D if we can find a homomorphism from T(D) to T(C)
All objects satisfying T(C) also satisfy T(D) thus all objects in Ci belong to Di. This is a subsumption
Ci ⊆ Di
C and D are equivalent if they are subsumed by each other in both directions
 

The terminological box (Tbox) :
The Tbox imposes restrictions to interpretation of concepts
Interpretations that do not satisfy this are ignored
A General concept inclusion GCI is an expression C ⊑ D
A Tbox is a finite set of these GCIs, implying a finite set of restrictions.

Tbox semantics : 
An interpretation I satisfies the GCI C subsumed by D is Ci subsumes Di
I is a model of the Tbox if it satisfies all GCIs in Tbox T

Tbox reasoning : 
Tbox reasoning is restricted to the class of models only
Concept satisfiability, if their exists a model I of T such that the set Ci is non empty
Concept subsumption (with respect to T) does Ci ⊆ Di hold for all models i of T

 
Note that every EL Tbox is consistent given that there are no contradictions. This results in that Every EL concept is satisfiable with respect to an EL Tbox
Normal form :
A GCI is in normal form if it has one of these shapes.
A ⊑ B
A1 ∏ A2 ⊑ B
A ⊑ ∃ r.B
∃r.A ⊑ B
Somewhat of an atomic syntax
A Tbox is in normal form if all the GCIs are in normal form
C ⊑ D ∏ E
Is equivalent to
C ⊑ D and C ⊑ E
